esponse to the  prediction of independent action and  among the clinical trials that we looked  at many of them were beneficial and  approved combinations even without  synergistic effects so if these sorts of  large synergistic interactions could  translate into human Krauser  in the future we could see some really  beneficial responses to combination  therapies if we can find the right sets  of drugs and the right patient  populations where synergistic  interactions will truly occur there is  both some cause for caution here a need  to be very careful about how we measure  synergy in preclinical settings because  in most of the clinical trials that I  presented there were experiments in cell  lines or mouse models that did show  evidence of synergistic interactions  which somehow did not manifest in the  human clinical trial data one example of  this comes from experiments in  colorectal cancer cell lines treated  with sort of somatic and oxaliplatin in  cell lines they showed a very strong  degree of synergistic effect a  combination index of 0.1 meaning the  combination was 10 times more potent in  the dose response function than the  single agents and when that cell line  that showed a synergistic interaction  was implanted into a mouse xenograft  the combination of the therapy  illustrated in blue the blue growth  curve here on the right was far superior  to the activity of either of the single  agents shown in yellow and red now if we  compare the activity of this combination  therapy in human clinical trials in  chaos wild-type colorectal cancer we can  see on the bottom right hand side here a  curve in pink which shows the survival  benefit of treating patients with  Satoshi map mono therapy in the case of  patients whose cancer was refractory to  chemotherapy so after chemotherapy  failed they received so toxin n by  itself and this provided some  improvement to survival so focusing that  was then tested in much larger clinical  trials combining it with co